  "worked_solution": "Using the fact that $6=\\frac{6}{1}$, we can write the expression as\n\n$$\n\\frac{3w}{w+4}-\\frac{6}{1}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn order to subtract the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. So, we put both fractions over a common denominator of $w+4$ by multiplying the top and bottom of the second fraction by $w+4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3w}{w+4}-\\frac{6}{1} & = \\\\ \\frac{3w}{w+4}-\\frac{w+4}{w+4}\\cdot \\frac{6}{1} & = \\\\ \\frac{3w}{w+4}-\\frac{6(w+4)}{w+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract the fractions and simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3w}{w+4}-\\frac{6(w+4)}{w+4} & = \\\\ \\frac{3w-6(w+4)}{w+4} & = \\\\ \\frac{3w-6w-24}{w+4} & = \\\\ \\frac{-3w-24}{w+4} & = \\\\ -\\frac{3(w+8)}{w+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
